<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#afrikaans">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afrikaans</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Afrikaans is a West Germanic language that developed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> spoken by settlers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, with influence from Malay, Portuguese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe</a> and other languages. It was recognised as an official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in place of Dutch in 1925. Today it is one of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s official languages and is also widely spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afrikaans.jpg" alt="Afrikaans" width="285" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fobos92 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Idioma_afrik%C3%A1ans.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
